\section*{AP \({ }^{\text {® }}\) CALCULUS AB \\ 2011 SCORING GUIDELINES (Form B)}

\section*{Question 3}

The functions \(f\) and \(g\) are given by \(f(x)=\sqrt{x}\) and \(g(x)=6-x\). Let \(R\) be the region bounded by the \(x\)-axis and the graphs of \(f\) and \(g\), as shown in the figure above.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find the area of \(R\).
\item[(b)] The region \(R\) is the base of a solid. For each \(y\), where \(0 \leq y \leq 2\), the cross section of the solid taken perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis is a rectangle whose base lies in \(R\) and whose height is \(2 y\). Write, but do not evaluate, an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid.
\item[(c)] There is a point \(P\) on the graph of \(f\) at which the line tangent to the graph of \(f\) is perpendicular to the graph of \(g\). Find the coordinates of point \(P\).
\item[(a)] Area \(=\int_{0}^{4} \sqrt{x} d x+\frac{1}{2} \cdot 2 \cdot 2=\left.\frac{2}{3} x^{3 / 2}\right|_{x=0} ^{x=4}+2=\frac{22}{3}\)
\item[(b)] \(y=\sqrt{x} \Rightarrow x=y^{2}\)
\[
\begin{aligned}
& y=6-x \Rightarrow x=6-y \\
& \text { Width }=(6-y)-y^{2} \\
& \text { Volume }=\int_{0}^{2} 2 y\left(6-y-y^{2}\right) d y
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { integral } \\ 1: \text { antiderivative } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}2: \text { integrand } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: f^{\prime}(x) \\ 1: \text { equation } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[(c)] \(g^{\prime}(x)=-1\)
Thus a line perpendicular to the graph of \(g\) has slope 1.
\[
\begin{aligned}
& f^{\prime}(x)=\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}} \\
& \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}}=1 \Rightarrow x=\frac{1}{4}
\end{aligned}
\]
The point \(P\) has coordinates \(\left(\frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{2}\right)\).
\end{itemize}
